{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mummy-the-curse/103135-after-reading-the-first-five-chapters-of-guildhalls","id":103135,"title":"After reading the first five chapters of Guildhalls","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mummy: The Curse"],"posts":[{"date":"2014-03-31T21:17:08-07:00","id":103135,"title":"After reading the first five chapters of Guildhalls","content":"I read the guild chapters of Guildhalls and I could sense a sense of interest and hubris in them.<br /> <br /> The Maa-Kep seek unity in guild politics, though I think that their people skills need work since they seek to put the world's nations under the yoke of Iremian thought. Plus, what is the title for Qathars for the other denizens. Since, the Maa-Kep seek cultural unity, I would think that Kindred and Uratha would be part of that, since the Mehket probably resided in Irem during the First Nights, unless the Kindred were after Irem's disappearance.<br /> <br /> I liked the Born of Gold because of their dedication to Dedwin, though with the other denizens of the World of Darkness, they see them as inferior to their own abilities.<br /> <br /> No matter how I spin it, the Sesha-Nebu or Closed Books give off a sort of Slytherin-ish vibe when I read their goals. They alter reality on the Scroll of Ages to stroke their egos, they call Maat a lie, even though all the guilds practice it without realizing it. They also seek to recover the lost magics of Will, and considering that the Closed Books hate the other guilds for one reason or another, I see two results if that happens:<br /> <br /> 1. Using their new powers they turn the other guilds into subguilds that they lord over.<br /> <br /> 2. The Closed Books host a Red Wedding with the other guilds, making them the only viable guild.<br /> <br /> With the Su-Menant, I feel pity for them. After centuries of relevance, they are this close to becoming an ex-guild, and the threat of the Red Magician is preventing more progressive members of the guild to help them out of this problem.<br /> <br /> And finally with the Tef Aahbi, they seem very good at utilizing the latest methods, though their hubris is that they focus on the project so much that they lose sight of everything around them.<br /> <br /> So, yeah. And why is it that the Born of Gold and the Closed Books are the only guilds that seem to have any experience with the other supers according to the book?<br /> <br /> Can't wait to see the new Utterances and stuff.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/891-jackalopevegas","name":"JackalopeVegas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-03-31T22:31:55-07:00","id":103182,"title":"","content":"1)The Mehket are implied, quite often, to have origins from around the time of Ahkenaten and correspond with the fall of the Cult of Set(Or transformation, if you will), well after Irem. The same must be said about the Julii with Remus, the Gangrel with either Remus or some Gaulish origin story, and the Daeva with Lillith from Mesopotamia. The Nosferatru might have been there, though-there's roots are the cloudiest of the bunch.<br /> <br /> 2)Well, with the Maa-Kep and Su-Menent focused on the purity of Irem and the Tef-Aabhi focused on their own ego-maniacal plan for the world that may or may not include Irem, only the Mesen-Nebu and the Sesha-Hebsu <i>would</i> focus on them-Dedwen knows no bounds and draws from all, and information is likewise valuable no matter it's source.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2014-04-01T14:13:54-07:00","id":103633,"title":"","content":"I disagree with you on Maat. I don't think the Closed Book see it as a 'lie'; that would be giving it too much respect. They think it's a silly notion created by a degenerate successor society to Irem.<br /> <br /> Also, at least as the Deathless see it, Maat is a concept of there being some kind of benevolent traditional order to society. That's completely absent from Irem; being as it is a very recent creation based blatantly upon the mass-enslavement of conquered peoples. The Arisen certainly believe that there's an order that should be upheld but the exact reason is kinda fuzzy, and it's not exactly benevolent.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}